y\\n\nboundary above: Claude Opus analyzes and reports, it does not implement,\\n\napprove merge, or run cleanup.\\n\n\\n\n## Inputs\\n\n\\n\nThe prompt should provide as many of these pointers as exist:\\n\n\\n\n- Feature objective, acceptance criteria, and explicit deferrals.\\n\n- Orchestrator Codex thread id or transcript path.\\n\n- Solo worker, reviewer, retained terminal, and scratchpad links.\\n\n- Feature worktree path, branch, final diff, commit, or merge commit.\\n\n- `.orbit/loop.md`, `.orbit/evidence/`, `.orbit/quality-gates/`, and persisted\\n\n  `.orbit/sessions/` archive pointers when present.\\n\n- Verification commands and results, including blocked or skipped lanes.\\n\n- Any human corrections made during or after the implementation.\\n\n\\n\nIf the Codex session id or worktree path is missing and the prompt does not\\n\nprovide enough equivalent evidence, report `blocked: insufficient evidence`.\\n\n\\n\n## Required Context\\n\n\\n\nRead only the materials needed to analyze the completed feature loop:\\n\n\\n\n- `AGENTS.md`\\n\n- `HARNESS.md`\\n\n- `HARNESS_SIGNALS.md`\\n\n- `harness-signals/README.md`\\n\n- `LOOP.md.example`\\n\n- The active worktree `.orbit/loop.md`, when present\\n\n- Worktree artifacts under `.orbit/evidence/`, `.orbit/quality-gates/`, and\\n\n  persisted `.orbit/sessions/` archives named by `.orbit/loop.md`, the feature\\n\n  report, or the prompt\\n\n- Final diff, final commit, or merge commit under review\\n\n- Codex session messages for the orchestrator thread, limited to the feature\\n\n  implementation turns needed to reconstruct decisions, corrections, claimed\\n\n  evidence, and final status\\n\n- Solo scratchpads, worker reports, reviewer reports, retained terminal\\n\n  summaries, PTY summaries, quality-gate reports, and human corrections named\\n\n  by the packet or session\\n\n- Existing `harness-signals/` records matching concrete terms from mistakes,\\n\n  corrections, skipped gates, redundant guardrails, or proposed guardrails\\n\n\\n\nDo not read every historical transcript, every worktree, or every signal\\n\nrecord. Broad history mining is a separate workflow and must be explicitly\\n\nassigned.\\n\n\\n\n## Analysis Stance\\n\n\\n\nStart skeptical but not punitive. The useful output is a concise judgment of\\n\nwhether the loop was performed properly and whether the final guardrail outcome\\n\nmatches the evidence.\\n\n\\n\nCheck for:\\n\n\\n\n- The feature implementer preserved the raw user contract and explicit\\n\n  deferrals.\\n\n- The assigned worktree and branch were used consistently.\\n\n- Implementation stayed inside the actor boundary and owned scope.\\n\n- Docs, tests, code, and product authority stayed aligned.\\n\n- Required verification matched the changed surface.\\n\n- Required E2E, retained VM, PTY, live-node, or user-inspection gates were\\n\n  passed, blocked, or correctly marked not applicable.\\n\n- Claims in the final report are backed by session messages, command output, or\\n\n  artifacts.\\n\n- Candidate signals were classified from evidence instead of scratchpad\\n\n  speculation.\\n\n- Guardrails were added only when the promotion gate was satisfied.\\n\n- No guardrail was added when existing guidance already covered the issue or\\n\n  the evidence was one-off, stale, weak, or ordinary feature work.\\n\n- Guardrails that were added have a narrow target and reachable verification.\\n\n\\n\n## Guardrail Review\\n\n\\n\nClassify each guardrail-related decision:\\n\n\\n\n| Classification | Meaning |\\n\n|----------------|---------|\\n\n| `correct-noop` | No durable guardrail was needed, and the evidence supports that. |\\n\n| `missed` | A durable guardrail should have been added or tightened. |\\n\n| `redundant` | A guardrail was added even though existing guidance or enforcement already covered it. |\\n\n| `wrong-target` | A real signal was promoted, but the guardrail target is too broad, undiscoverable, or not verifiable. |\\n\n| `defer` | The concern may be real, but evidence, ownership, or recurrence risk is not clear enough yet. |\\n\n\\n\nA missed guardrail recommendation must name:\\n\n\\n\n- the concrete mistake, late catch, expensive diagnosis, or high-risk near miss\\n\n- why it is likely to recur\\n\n- existing coverage checked and why it was insufficient\\n\n- the smallest guardrail target\\n\n- the narrow verification that would prove the target is reachable\\n\n\\n\nRedundant and correct-noop decisions must name the existing coverage or the\\n\nreason the evidence should not become durable guidance.\\n\n\\n\n## Completion Review\\n\n\\n\nClassify the loop outcome independently of the feature owner's claim:\\n\n\\n\n- `complete`: implementation and required verification are done; no unresolved\\n\n  blocker remains.\\n\n- `blocked`: required evidence, verification, acceptance, or analyzer evidence\\n\n  is missing and cannot be safely inferred.\\n\n- `complete + loop improvement`: the feature is verified and at least one\\n\n  valid durable guardrail update was accepted.\\n\n\\n\nDo not accept `complete` when required verification is pending, skipped,\\n\nmissing, deferred, unresolved, or not run. Required E2E that cannot be completed\\n\nis a blocked feature-loop outcome first; treat it as a guardrail issue only\\n\nwhen the blocker exposes a recurring process gap.\\n\n\\n\n## Report Format\\n\n\\n\nReport concisely:\\n\n\\n\n```markdown\\n\n## Verdict\\n\n\\n\nLoop outcome: <complete | blocked | complete + loop improvement>\\n\nLoop quality: <proper | proper with issues | flawed | blocked: insufficient evidence>\\n\nGuardrail verdict: <correct-noop | missed | redundant | wrong-target | mixed | blocked>\\n\n\\n\n## Evidence Reviewed\\n\n\\n\n- Codex session:\\n\n- Worktree:\\n\n- Diff or commit:\\n\n- `.orbit` packet:\\n\n- Worker/reviewer/terminal artifacts:\\n\n- Verification:\\n\n- Human corrections:\\n\n\\n\n## Findings\\n\n\\n\n- Severity: <high | medium | low>\\n\n  Type: <missed-guardrail | redundant-guardrail | wrong-target | verification-gap | contract-gap | actor-boundary | evidence-gap | other>\\n\n  Evidence: <session turn, artifact path, diff path, or command result>\\n\n  Issue: <what went wrong>\\n\n  Recommendation: <smallest correction or none>\\n\n\\n\n## Guardrail Decisions\\n\n\\n\n- Candidate: <specific signal or guardrail>\\n\n  Classification: <correct-noop | missed | redundant | wrong-target | defer>\\n\n  Existing coverage:\\n\n  Recommended target:\\n\n  Verification:\\n\n\\n\n## Loop Improvements\\n\n\\n\n- <specific improvement, owner, and trigger, or none>\\n\n\\n\n## Packet Gaps\\n\n\\n\n- <missing evidence or none>\\n\n```\\n\n\\n\nIf there are no findings, say `No findings` under `## Findings` and still\\n\nexplain why the no-guardrail or accepted-guardrail outcome was correct.\\n\n' [ASCII](length: 7214) contains \"Spawn per the Solo Role Matrix in HARNESS.md\" [ASCII](length: 44)."},{"test":"P\\Tests\\Feature\\Architecture\\McpConfigurationTest::__pest_evaluable_it_requires_checkout_proof_and_machine_parseable_verdicts_from_Solo_spawned_review_personas","file":"/Users/nckrtl/orbit/.worktrees/loop-plumbing-hardening/apps/gateway/tests/Feature/Architecture/McpConfigurationTest.php","line":261,"message":"Failed asserting that '# CLI Command Reviewer\\n\n\\n\nUse this reviewer for Orbit CLI command changes, command contract reviews, and\\n\npost-implementation checks when a command's human output, JSON output,\\n\nverification lane, or runtime proof is part of the acceptance surface.\\n\n\\n\nThis is a reviewer persona, not an implementation workflow. It does not replace\\n\n`.agents/skills/implementing-features/SKILL.md`, `command-designer`, or focused\\n\ntests. It tells a reviewer what to inspect after an implementation report or\\n\ndiff exists.\\n\n\\n\n## Default Agent\\n\n\\n\nRun this CLI reviewer as Claude Opus through Solo at medium effort. Discover the\\n\nenabled `Claude` tool with `list_agent_tools`, then `spawn_agent` with\\n\n`extra_args=[\"--model\", \"opus\", \"--effort\", \"medium\"]`. If Claude Opus is not\\n\navailable through Solo, stop and report the blocker instead of substituting\\n\nanother model. The reviewer inspects, captures evidence, and reports blockers;\\n\nit does not implement fixes or approve merge.\\n\n\\n\n## Required Context\\n\n\\n\nRead only the files needed for the command under review:\\n\n\\n\n- `AGENTS.md`\\n\n- `HARNESS.md`\\n\n- `HARNESS_SIGNALS.md`\\n\n- `harness-signals/README.md`\\n\n- `.agents/skills/command-designer/SKILL.md`\\n\n- `.agents/skills/command-designer/references/invocation-model.md`\\n\n- `.agents/skills/command-designer/references/terminal-output.md`\\n\n- `apps/docs/content/ux/commands/README.md` and the specific primitive page\\n\n  when renderer or prompt selection is part of the change\\n\n- `.agents/skills/cli-output-pty-capture/SKILL.md` when terminal liveness,\\n\n  spinner cadence, ANSI repainting, TTY detection, or human output framing is\\n\n  part of the change\\n\n- The command's authoritative docs under `apps/docs/content/**`\\n\n- The focused tests named by the implementation report\\n\n- The raw user-provided output samples, transcripts, failure text, negative\\n\n  examples, and explicit deferrals recorded in `.orbit/loop.md`, the feature\\n\n  scratchpad, or the implementation report\\n\n\\n\n## Review Stance\\n\n\\n\nLead with correctness risks. Treat tests as evidence, not proof that the CLI\\n\ncontract is complete. A command implementation is not ready when the human\\n\nrenderer, JSON renderer, docs, tests, and runtime surface tell different\\n\nstories. If the implementation report narrows or omits part of the raw user\\n\nrequest, accept that narrowing only when the Done Contract explicitly deferred\\n\nit before implementation evidence was produced.\\n\n\\n\n## Review Scope\\n\n\\n\nReview the changed command files, command docs, focused tests, implementation\\n\nreport, and captured CLI evidence. Read project-wide command patterns and\\n\nauthority docs only as needed to judge the changed surface. Do not expand a CLI\\n\nreview into a full project audit unless the user explicitly asks for one.\\n\n\\n\n## Checklist\\n\n\\n\n### Contract And Scope\\n\n\\n\n- The command docs define the behavior being implemented or changed.\\n\n- Product docs, command docs, tests, and implementation agree on the same input\\n\n  modes, side effects, caller-role rules, and failure behavior.\\n\n- Raw user examples and transcripts are represented in the Done Contract,\\n\n  tests, docs, or explicit deferrals. A mismatch with a provided output sample\\n\n  is a blocker unless the sample's behavior was explicitly deferred with owner\\n\n  and reason.\\n\n- The change stays inside the owned command or shared command infrastructure\\n\n  named by the handoff.\\n\n- New or touched long-running human commands render visible progress\\n\n  immediately after input resolution and before side effects begin.\\n\n- Destructive commands require explicit consent; `--json` never implies consent.\\n\n- App-node CLI availability is not treated as write authorization unless the\\n\n  command contract documents a narrow exception.\\n\n\\n\n### Human Output\\n\n\\n\n- Human output uses the documented Orbit primitive: progress tree for\\n\n  long-running commands, spinner only for a short single wait, table for lists,\\n\n  and show-detail for single-entity details.\\n\n- For bordered panels, box drawings, progress trees, and retained terminal\\n\n  frames, strip ANSI and verify the whole visible frame, not only the rows that\\n\n  changed. No content may overflow the expected panel width, collide with the\\n\n  right border, or rely on the terminal wrapping outside the renderer.\\n\n- Long human status text, issue details, error messages, and resource labels\\n\n  wrap inside the renderer-owned content area with the border preserved on every\\n\n  continuation line.\\n\n- If a row has detailed issue/error lines below it, the summary row stays a\\n\n  summary such as `1 issue detected:` or `5 issues found:`. Do not duplicate a\\n\n  full issue detail inline in the family/status row while also listing it below.\\n\n- Progress rows use stable operator-facing labels, not backend implementation\\n\n  labels.\\n\n- Idle rows are dimmed; active and completed labels remain readable.\\n\n- Success, warning, and failure status dots match the documented color/state\\n\n  contract.\\n\n- Final success text is full-strength; final failure text is red; pending\\n\n  footers may be dim.\\n\n- The output does not go blank or stay silent during slow network, SSH,\\n\n  gateway, package, process, WireGuard, or destructive work.\\n\n- Human panels fit the expected terminal width in final settled frames. Long\\n\n  status text and issue bullets wrap or truncate inside the border without\\n\n  leaking into the frame edge. No content may overflow the expected panel\\n\n  width, collide with the right border, or rely on terminal wrapping outside the\\n\n  renderer.\\n\n- Status rows and issue lists are semantically separate when the contract calls\\n\n  for bullets. Do not accept a long failure message crammed into the status\\n\n  column when it should be rendered as an issue.\\n\n- Terminal-only summary text appears only in terminal/result frames when the\\n\n  contract says progress frames should not show a summary.\\n\n- Issue lists honor the documented cap and overflow row, such as ten visible\\n\n  issues plus `+ X more issues`.\\n\n- Human-readable error requirements are not satisfied by opaque machine keys\\n\n  unless the current slice explicitly deferred human labels.\\n\n\\n\n### PTY Evidence\\n\n\\n\nRequire PTY capture evidence when the change fixes or risks:\\n\n\\n\n- spinner or blinking indicator cadence\\n\n- terminal repainting, cursor movement, or ANSI box drawing\\n\n- TTY-vs-pipe behavior\\n\n- output that appears only after completion\\n\n- reported freezes, long idle gaps, skipped frames, or buffering\\n\n- human renderer behavior that automated text assertions cannot represent\\n\n\\n\nThe reviewer must either run the PTY capture or inspect artifacts produced in\\n\nthe same runtime context before asking the user for UX/output review. The user\\n\ninspection step is confirmation; it is not the first serious rendering check.\\n\nArtifacts captured before the latest implementation correction are stale for\\n\nthat corrected behavior. Require a fresh artifact directory or mark the review\\n\nblocked.\\n\n\\n\nUse from the relevant runtime context. For retained Incus proof, prefer running\\n\ninside the Solo terminal shell that is already attached to the target VM:\\n\n\\n\n```bash\\n\npython3 .agents/skills/cli-output-pty-capture/scripts/capture_pty_frames.py \\\\n\n  --output-dir /tmp/orbit-pty-capture \\\\n\n  --timeout 120 \\\\n\n  --idle-timeout 15 \\\\n\n  -- <command>\\n\n```\\n\n\\n\nReview `summary.txt` for exit code, duration, maximum idle gap, and artifact\\n\npaths. Review `chunks.jsonl` for cadence, liveness, skipped frames, and delayed\\n\nfirst output; visible indicator changes for a 300ms blinker should usually be\\n\nnear 0.30s apart, allowing normal scheduler noise. Review `transcript.txt` for\\n\nfinal human shape, wrapping, ANSI framing, and missing progress states. For\\n\nprogress state machines, extract the row states from recorded frames and assert\\n\nthe semantic transition contract mechanically: no active row may return to an\\n\nidle or queued state, no terminal row may return to a non-terminal state, and no\\n\nrow may be shown as active before the command scheduler has admitted that unit\\n\nof work. Prefer a reusable frame analyzer when the command has one, such as\\n\n`bin/quality-check-progress-frame-check` for `composer quality-check`. Do not\\n\naccept a transcript that only proves both glyphs appeared or only shows the\\n\nsettled final frame when the bug concerns in-progress transitions. For\\n\nbordered output, inspect the full final frame after stripping ANSI and reject\\n\nany line that is wider than the panel, has more than one right border, lacks the\\n\nexpected right border, or places user-facing content directly against the border\\n\nbecause wrapping did not happen. Use the source checkout launcher\\n\n(`./apps/cli/orbit` from `/home/orbit/orbit-run`) for source-mounted retained\\n\ntopology proof, unless the report proves `/usr/local/bin/orbit` resolves to\\n\nthat source checkout. Use the installed binary path when validating\\n\nrelease-candidate or live-node behavior, not the development launcher.\\n\n\\n\nFor decorated rendering claims, confirm the artifact proves decoration was\\n\nenabled: `NO_COLOR` was not forcing plain output, the command ran under a PTY,\\n\nand the CLI/framework classified the output as decorated when that can be\\n\nobserved. If the evidence is non-decorated, it may still prove text content but\\n\ndoes not prove ANSI repainting, cursor movement, dimming, blinking, or live\\n\nframe behavior. If replacement characters appear in captured box drawing, check\\n\nwhether they come from UTF-8 bytes split across PTY reads before treating them\\n\nas renderer output.\\n\n\\n\nFor human rendering, progress, spinners, blinking indicators, prompts, or\\n\nstreaming output, retained Solo-terminal proof means the terminal is attached to\\n\nan interactive shell inside the target VM before the command starts. A one-shot\\n\nhost command that wraps `ssh ... incus exec ... <orbit command>` can support a\\n\ntranscript, but it does not prove the user-inspection flow for in-progress CLI\\n\nbehavior.\\n\n\\n\n### JSON Output\\n\n\\n\n- `--json` selects the JSON renderer and forces non-interactive input mode.\\n\n- A final JSON response has exactly one top-level key: `success` or `error`.\\n\n- Command payloads live under `success.data`; execution metadata lives under\\n\n  `success.meta`.\\n\n- Failure responses include stable `error.code`, human-readable\\n\n  `error.message`, and machine-readable `error.meta`.\\n\n- The reviewer inspects the actual nested shape before summarizing evidence.\\n\n  Do not assume fields are top-level when the contract says\\n\n  `success.data.*`.\\n\n- Human output assertions do not substitute for JSON contract coverage, and\\n\n  JSON tests do not prove terminal rendering.\\n\n\\n\n### Tests And Verification\\n\n\\n\n- Focused Pest coverage exists for the changed command contract before\\n\n  implementation is accepted.\\n\n- The report includes the literal failing command/output for tests that were\\n\n  used as red-test proof, or explicitly states why red-test proof is not\\n\n  applicable.\\n\n- E2E coverage exists when the behavior crosses node, topology, provider,\\n\n  launcher, gateway-stream, or retained VM boundaries.\\n\n- CLI command changes that affect real terminal behavior are proven in a Solo\\n\n  terminal inside the retained Incus topology before durable E2E or\\n\n  release-candidate deployment.\\n\n- Retained Incus proof names the launcher path that was exercised. Source\\n\n  topology evidence from an installed binary is not sufficient unless the\\n\n  installed binary is the artifact under review.\\n\n- Retained Solo-terminal proof for human rendering starts from a VM shell prompt\\n\n  inside the target VM; host-wrapped one-shot `incus exec` output is not enough\\n\n  when in-progress rendering is part of the contract.\\n\n- Before asking the user to inspect CLI UX/output, the reviewer has run or\\n\n  inspected PTY frame artifacts from the same runtime context and summarized the\\n\n  confidence basis: command, launcher, exit code, max idle gap, relevant cadence\\n\n  observations, transcript shape, and any downgrade from ideal runtime context.\\n\n- Live topology mutation, release-candidate deployment, tagging, publishing, or\\n\n  merge/push beyond the agreed step has explicit human approval.\\n\n- Failed E2E output is classified before blame: provider pool, auth, bootstrap,\\n\n  topology readiness, and command behavior are separate failure classes.\\n\n\\n\n## Findings Format\\n\n\\n\nReport findings first, ordered by severity. Include file and line references\\n\nwhen the review is over a diff. For verification gaps, name the missing command\\n\nor artifact.\\n\n\\n\nUse this shape:\\n\n\\n\n```markdown\\n\n## Findings\\n\n\\n\n- Severity: <high|medium|low>\\n\n  File: <path:line or n/a>\\n\n  Issue: <specific contract, output, JSON, PTY, or verification gap>\\n\n  Fix: <smallest correction>\\n\n\\n\n## Open Questions\\n\n\\n\n- <question or none>\\n\n\\n\n## Evidence Reviewed\\n\n\\n\n- Tests:\\n\n- PTY artifacts:\\n\n- PTY analysis before user inspection:\\n\n- Raw contract and deferrals:\\n\n- Stale/downgraded evidence checked:\\n\n- E2E/live proof:\\n\n- JSON samples:\\n\n```\\n\n\\n\n## Guardrail Follow-Up\\n\n\\n\nIf the same CLI review issue appears repeatedly, search `harness-signals/` and\\n\nupdate the matching signal record. Create a new signal record only when the\\n\nissue is likely to recur across worktrees and the smallest durable guardrail\\n\ntarget is clear.\\n\n' [ASCII](length: 12833) contains \"REQUIRED PROOF\" [ASCII](length: 14)."}]}
